### How do you identify the tenant?

Tenant identification must be reliable and happen early. The JWT claim is the strongest source because it is signed and cannot be forged.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.filter;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GlobalFilter;
import org.springframework.core.Ordered;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Component
public class TenantResolutionFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        // Derive tenant from JWT claim (signed, cannot be forged)
        String jwtTenant = extractTenantFromJwt(exchange);
        if (jwtTenant == null) {
            // Fail closed: no tenant → reject
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }

        // Check if client-supplied header matches JWT
        String headerTenant = exchange.getRequest().getHeaders()
                .getFirst("X-Tenant-Id");
        if (headerTenant != null && !headerTenant.equals(jwtTenant)) {
            // Spoof attempt: header says one tenant, JWT says another
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }

        // Stamp verified tenant on the request
        exchange.getRequest().mutate()
                .header("X-Tenant-Id", jwtTenant);
        return chain.filter(exchange);
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        return -90; // Run early, after auth
    }

    private String extractTenantFromJwt(ServerWebExchange exchange) {
        // Extract "tenant" claim from validated JWT
        return null; // implementation
    }
}
```

```text
// JWT claim: {sub: "user@partnerbank-a.com", tenant: "PARTNER-A"}
// Header: X-Tenant-Id: PARTNER-A → matches JWT → allowed
// Header: X-Tenant-Id: PARTNER-B → does NOT match JWT → REJECTED (spoof!)
// No tenant in JWT → REJECTED (fail closed)
```

### How do you propagate the tenant downstream?

The gateway injects the verified tenant as a trusted header. Downstream services read it to scope their data access.

```java
// Gateway sets: X-Tenant-Id: PARTNER-A (verified from JWT)
// Account Service reads:
String tenantId = request.getHeader("X-Tenant-Id");
List<Account> accounts = accountRepo.findByTenantId(tenantId);
// Only returns PARTNER-A's accounts, never PARTNER-B's
```

### How do per-tenant rate limits solve the noisy-neighbor problem?

Rate limits scoped to the tenant ensure one tenant's traffic surge cannot starve another.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.ratelimit.KeyResolver;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Configuration
public class TenantRateLimitConfig {

    @Bean
    public KeyResolver tenantKeyResolver() {
        // Rate limit per tenant, not per user
        return exchange -> Mono.justOrEmpty(
                exchange.getRequest().getHeaders().getFirst("X-Tenant-Id"));
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Per-tenant rate limit configuration
# PARTNER-A (premium): 1000 requests/second
# PARTNER-B (basic): 100 requests/second
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: accounts
          uri: lb://account-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/accounts/**
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                key-resolver: "#{@tenantKeyResolver}"
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 100
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 200
```

```text
// PARTNER-A sends 500 req/s: within their premium limit → all processed
// PARTNER-B sends 500 req/s: exceeds basic limit → excess gets 429
// PARTNER-A is unaffected by PARTNER-B's surge (noisy-neighbor solved)
```

### How does per-tenant routing work?

Different tenants can be routed to different backends for gradual rollouts or dedicated infrastructure.

```java
// Per-tenant routing:
// PARTNER-A (premium) → dedicated account-service instances
// PARTNER-B (basic) → shared account-service instances

// Route predicate:
// .route(r -> r
//     .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
